<p>&#8220;Plaindromes&#8221; are ordinary sentences that sound as if they ought to be palindromes:</p>
<p>Money-man I, an Adam, not even a doom.<br />
Stella, Otis deified Satan and Edna.<br />
An item? No, revolt took Natasha.<br />
Eggbert, a wry dross needs seating, tea-grams.<br />
Droop: an old fan? Needles fone a poor ode.</p>
<p>&#8220;Ungrams&#8221; are invalid anagrams:</p>
<p>Richard M. Nixon / Noxious charmer<br />
Brooklyn Bridge / Good ride by lake </p>
<p>Both were invented by <a href="https://digitalcommons.butler.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=2497&#038;context=wordways">Jef Raskin</a> of Cupertino, California (from the <a href="https://digitalcommons.butler.edu/wordways/vol43/iss3/">August 2010</a> issue of <a href="https://digitalcommons.butler.edu/wordways/"><em>Word Ways</em></a>).</p>
